        },
        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Dogs (primarily) and cats.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Any age:deciduous or permanent teeth can be involved.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Usually occurs in active animals that bite or chew a lot.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tooth is visibly broken with or having near pulpal exposure—90% of cases.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tooth may appear discolored.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tooth is not sensitive to percussion or cold or hot liquids or foods—note: acute tooth fracture with pulp exposure would be sensitive.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Facial swelling—usually localized but can spread, resulting in a cellulitis.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cutaneous sinus (draining tract) exuding pus—suppurative apical periodontitis:",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Suborbital abscess due to maxillary",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "fourth premolar involvement is a common presentation.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "On oral examination, especially of the maxilla, there may be a red, raised lesion (parulis) at the anticipated location of the tooth apex. Often present where the attached gingiva meets the alveolar mucosa (mucogingival line) or above.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Facial sensitivity may be slight but could be extensive if there is no draining.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tooth may be clinically asymptomatic, yet inflammation and bacteremia may be present.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tooth may be asymptomatic for a long time but will be affected eventually—subclinically or even years later.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In the absence of a fractured tooth with an exposed canal, a deep periodontal pocket may extend to the apex of one or more roots, resulting in a similar abscess.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May present as a jaw fracture, especially if the abscess affects the lower canine teeth or lower first molar. Slight trauma can cause a pathologic fracture of the mandible.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tooth may be loose and painful on palpation.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May have facial lymphadenitis.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Animal does not want to chew, especially on the affected side (plaque and calculus may accumulate), or will bite, but release quickly instead of holding on.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Some animals stop eating, the great",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "majority do not.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sinusitis—maxillary sinus is most commonly affected.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sense of smell (olfactory capabilities) may be affected, especially with drug, bomb, or food-sniffing working dogs.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Any pulpal trauma—direct blow with fracture of crown; chewing hard objects (carnassial teeth); defense (fighting—canine teeth); malocclusive trauma; tug-of-war; bone plating that damaged roots.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Advanced periodontal disease—a deep periodontal pocket can involve the apex where bacteria can enter the pulp system, especially at the palatal root of a small dog.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bacteria—the pulp can be affected by bacteria from dental caries, exposed dentinal tubules, or extension into endodontic system.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thermal heat resulting in pulpal necrosis— electrical cord burns, iatrogenic caused by use of electrocautery for gingival surgery, overpolishing during an oral hygiene procedure or use of rotary burs.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "DIAGNOSIS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "DIFFERENTIAL DIAGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Normal radiographic anatomy—chevron effect and mental formina.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tooth resorption—radiographs show no apical lucency or abscessation, but rather a disintegration of the root and/or crown with the loss of the periodontal ligament.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Oral tumor.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Squamous cell carcinoma and fibrosarcoma (rapidly growing and invasive) and ameloblastoma (slowly enlarges). These tumors can displace teeth, which often become mobile.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cementomas—radiographically show enlarged apical roots with a thin radiolucent zone continuous with the periodontal ligament.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cysts—radiographs usually show a very large lytic area involving any part of the root and/or unerupted crown:",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Radicular cysts, apical periodontal",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "granulomas.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 1,
              "text": "Dentigerous cyst—occurs from the",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "follicular cyst of an impacted or embedded tooth (usually the first mandibular premolars in dogs); in which case the radiograph show a tooth within the cyst.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Periapical scar—usually occurs on an endodontically treated tooth where there has been no further increase in the radiographic apical lucency after a 6-month post-treatment period.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Normal anatomy—mental foramens can be mistaken for apical lucencies on radiographic interpretation (the middle mental foramen just apical to second mandibular premolar).",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/BIOCHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "CBC may show a leukocytosis and/or a mild regenerative anemia.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CBC/CHEMISTRY/URINALYSIS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "OTHER LABORATORY TESTS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "IMAGING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "IMAGING",
          "canonical_heading": "IMAGING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Intraoral radiographs are the most commonly utilized diagnostic aid; demonstrates thickening of the apical periodontal ligament space; ill-defined radiolucency; shows bone loss at the apex as the lesion becomes chronic.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "As the lesion progresses, radiographic lesions consistent with osteomyelitis and cellulitis occur.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Must be differentiated from chevron effect:this is evident radiographically in the small teeth that commonly develop a tooth root abscess.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC PROCEDURES",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "If fistulization has occurred, can place a gutta percha cone into the sinus tract and take a radiograph to identify the affected tooth.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Transillumination with a strong fiber-optic light can help the clinician by distinguishing between a vital and necrotic pulp (compare to similar tooth on contralateral side).",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        },
        "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "DIAGNOSTIC TESTS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOLOGIC FINDINGS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Apical area has a central area of liquefaction necrosis containing disintegrating neutrophils and cellular debris, surrounded by macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells; can see bacteria.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Extension of the lesion into cancellous bone results in inflammation of the periapical bone and resorption.",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Chronic changes may develop tracts that can be lined with epithelium; osteomyelitis or cellulitis lesions may become fibrotic with a capsule (radicular cyst and/or a periapical periodontal granuloma).",
              "pdf_page": 1425,
              "printed_page": 1343,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    "TREATMENT": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Must surgically drain and remove the focus of infection.",
          "pdf_page": 1425,
          "printed_page": 1343,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Extraction of the tooth involved, with curettage of the apical infected area.",
          "pdf_page": 1425,
          "printed_page": 1343,
          "column": 3
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Chronic conditions require surgical removal of the granulation tissue and curettage of the tract.",
          "pdf_page": 1426,
          "printed_page": 1344,
          "column": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Endodontic treatment of the involved tooth (surgical root canal if apical legion large).",
          "pdf_page": 1426,
          "printed_page": 1344,
          "column": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Medical management can be instituted temporarily until surgery can be performed.",
          "pdf_page": 1426,
          "printed_page": 1344,
          "column": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "After treatment, cold packs on the area will help reduce inflammation.",
          "pdf_page": 1426,
          "printed_page": 1344,
          "column": 1
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Following extraction, oral rest is recommended: soft diet, no hard chew toys, until the gingiva is healed.",
          "pdf_page": 1426,
          "printed_page": 1344,
          "column": 1
        }
      ],
